      "claim": "Stone tool technologies (Oldowan, Acheulean, Levallois) have a continuous archaeological record extending back millions of years, far predating anatomically modern humans.",
      "evidence": "Oldowan tools date to ~2.6 million years ago; Acheulean handaxes to ~1.7 million years ago; Levallois techniques to ~300,000+ years ago (associated with H. heidelbergensis/Neanderthals).",
      "weight": 0.9,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Anatomically modern humans (AMH) emerged approximately 300,000 years ago, meaning any technology older than this predates AMH.",
      "evidence": "Fossil evidence from Jebel Irhoud and Omo Kibish dates AMH to ~300kya.",
      "weight": 0.8,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The Upper Paleolithic (Late Stone Age) began approximately 50,000 years ago, marking the period of 'behavioral modernity' where complex symbolic behaviors are typically dated.",
      "evidence": "Standard archaeological periodization places the start of the Upper Paleolithic around 50kya in Europe and later in other regions.",
      "weight": 0.8,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Elaborate burials, symbolic art, and large permanent settlements are primarily associated with the Upper Paleolithic or later periods, not the deep past.",
      "evidence": "Cave art (e.g., Chauvet) dates to ~30-40kya; elaborate burials are rare before the Middle/Upper Paleolithic; dense permanent villages are a Neolithic phenomenon (~10kya).",
      "weight": 0.7,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Improved stone tool technologies are the only option that demonstrably predates the Late Stone Age, the Upper Paleolithic, and anatomically modern humans.",
      "evidence": "Comparison of timelines: Stone tools (2.6m+ years) < AMH (300k years) < Upper Paleolithic (50k years). Options A, B, and D are largely confined to the Upper Paleolithic or later.",
